   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 7.4. Increased capacity of constituents to provide adequate labour protection to workers in diverse forms of work arrangements, including on digital labour platforms, and in informal employment</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Government established a Tripartite Technical Working group (TTWG) on domestic work in line with national policy (April 2021). The Ministry of Labour and Social Security (MLSS) coordinates the TTWG and includes: Workers Compensation Fund Control Board (WCFCB), National Pension Scheme Authority (NAPSA), Zambia Congress of Trade Unions (ZCTU), Domestic Workers Union of Zambia (DWUZ), United House for Domestic Workers Union of Zambia (UHDWUZ), Association of Employers of Domestic Workers in Zambia (AEDWZ), Zambia Federation of Employers (ZFE), and Labour Institute of Zambia. 
TTWG adopted recommendations to ratify C189 (Sept 2021), which were then approved by the Tripartite Consultative Labour Council (Oct 2021, to be confirmed). 
MLSS validated a rapid assessment of the impact of COVID-19 on the informal economy and domestic work (Aug 2021), leading to a TTWG campaign on COVID-19 measures in domestic work (Aug 2021). 
The Ministry of Finance and Development Planning integrated the findings to the Covid-19 Recovery Needs Analysis (CRNA), as part of the National Development Plan (Jan 2022). 
The TTWG validated a further qualitative assessment of the impact of COVID-19 on domestic work (Dec 2021 to be confirmed). TTWG developed and undertook an action plan and communications plan to promote formalization. NAPSA and MLSS developed and disseminated communication materials  resulting in 300 domestic workers registered to social security (Dec 2021 to be confirmed). DWUZ and UHDWUZ undertook campaigns on COVID-19 and formalization (2021). The ZCTU conducted community meetings and a training for maid centers on social security registration. DWUZ, UHDWUZ and AEDWZ increased their capacity to represent the sector during COVID-19, through a training on using digital conferencing platforms (Aug 2021). TTWG validated gender-responsive diagnosis of the behavioural drivers of informality in domestic work, and recommendations to formalize domestic work (Dec 2021). With support from Sweden, the ILO:
Facilitated and provided technical assistance to TTWG meetings 
Provided technical inputs to a TTWG workshop on the legal framework and possibility of ratifying C189 (Sept 2021)
Jointly, with UNDP, conducted a rapid assessment of the impact of COVID-19 on the informal economy, including domestic work, and commissioned further qualitative research on the impact of COVID-19 on domestic workers and their employers (Sept 2021). The ILO produced and submitted recommendations based on a summary of the findings of both assessments to the TTWG for validation (Dec. 2021, to be confirmed)
Coordinated and provided internet data bundles for a virtual National Planning Workshop on Domestic Work (May 2021)
Conducted a media strategy meeting to sensitize the media on the rights of domestic workers and ratification of C189. 14 outlets attended, of which 9 developed radio and print content on formalization of domestic work, and continue to act as change agents (June 2021).
Provided technical input and assistance to design, produce and distribute TTWG communication materials (Aug 2021)
Provided technical assistance to the ZCTU monthly community meetings with domestic workers, and training for maid centers, on NAPSA and WCFCB registration processes. The ILO facilitated media coverage of the maid center training, which aired on ZNBC radio 2 and Millennium Radio (Aug 2021)
Provided the unions with internet bundles and training for them on how to use online conferencing software to build their capacity to represent domestic workers in the TTWG (Aug 2021); and provided technical assistance to increase their visibility online, in times of COVID-19, through Facebook and Google (Sept 2021)
Commissioned a behavioural diagnostic and experiment to generate insights on the behavioural drivers of informality and recommendations to formalize domestic work through registration to social security.</narrative>
   </description>
